From 4e2eb4d8c23e2f90b2d8e465c8b49a831b227bbf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tasker <tasker@localhost>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 08:01:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] hw/audio: intel-hda-ace: the pipeline table (create,
 set-state, delete)

Once the firmware runs, the three pipeline messages of IPC4 -- create
pipeline (17), set pipeline state (19) and delete pipeline (18) -- are
parsed as the IPC4 header lays them out (instance at primary 23:16,
priority at 15:11, memory pages at 10:0; core and the low-power flag in
the extension; the state at primary 15:0), kept in a table by instance id
and answered after the FW_CONFIG reply's delay with the request's type,
the reply direction and a status.

The plan (P3, 5.5 and 8 C1) gives one rule of the firmware's state machine,
which the model judges: a pipeline must be paused before it can run, and
any other pipeline asked to run answers status 5.  Every other transition
is tracked as asked (the plan gives no rule for it: INFO_NEEDED).  The
answers the plan does not fix are model values: a set-state or delete of a
pipeline that does not exist answers 9 (IPC4_INVALID_RESOURCE_ID), a second
create of an id answers 10 (IPC4_RESOURCE_ID_EXISTS), an instance id of 0
(the plan's R-G) or past the pipeline count of the FW_CONFIG reply answers 1
(IPC4_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM), the multi-pipeline form of set-state answers 7
(IPC4_INVALID_REQUEST), and a new pipeline is tracked as uninitialized.

The table starts empty with every firmware start and goes with its power.
Every reply is now traced decoded with its type and status (ace_ipc_msg),
and a request a fault drops is traced with its type, so a log shows every
message the host sent and what the firmware answered.
